<Project>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    #1815: pin the RUNTIME PACK version for self-contained / Native-AOT publishes
    ============================================================================

    THE PROBLEM
    The runtime packs baked into every self-contained / Native-AOT publish
    (Microsoft.NETCore.App.Runtime.*, Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App.Runtime.*,
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.App.Runtime.*, and the Native-AOT ILCompiler +
    Microsoft.NETCore.App.Runtime.NativeAOT.* packs) are NOT pinned by anything
    the repo controls. Their version is the resolving SDK's KnownFrameworkReference
    / KnownILCompilerPack metadata — i.e. whatever global.json's "10.0.203" +
    rollForward:latestFeature happens to resolve on the building machine.
    latestFeature picks the highest INSTALLED feature band, and a band whose VS
    release has left support does not track the monthly runtime patch. On the dev
    box that resolves SDK 10.0.204, whose bundled runtime is 10.0.8 — which 9
    advisories flag as vulnerable (6 high, incl. CVE-2026-62901; first patched
    10.0.11). So local self-contained publishes of the agent / broker / shim
    (hot-deploy loop, PSDirect upgrades, golden-VHDX tooling) silently shipped a
    vulnerable runtime; CI only landed on 10.0.11 by luck of which SDK bands were
    installed.

    THE FIX (Option A of #1815)
    Pin the runtime-pack version to the current patched runtime so the SDK
    downloads that EXACT pack regardless of which SDK band is resolving. Every
    self-contained / AOT publish is now deterministic and Dependabot-legible. A
    build-time floor assertion (target below) then PROVES the pin actually took
    effect, so a stale value or a silently no-op'd Update fails the build loudly
    instead of quietly shipping a vulnerable runtime again.

    WHY NOT THE $(RuntimeFrameworkVersion) PROPERTY
    The obvious lever, <RuntimeFrameworkVersion>, over-reaches on the Windows-
    targeting projects (Wep.Agent, Wep.Detonation.Broker, ... are
    net10.0-windows): the SDK applies it to EVERY framework reference, including
    Microsoft.Windows.SDK.NET.Ref, whose real version is a Windows build number
    (10.0.26100.57), NOT a 10.0.x runtime version. That produces a duplicate
    Microsoft.Windows.SDK.NET.Ref PackageDownload ([10.0.11] + [10.0.26100.57])
    and fails restore with NU1505 (Warning-As-Error under the repo analyzers).
    So instead we pin the three RUNTIME-pack framework references directly, which
    leaves the Windows SDK ref pack untouched. The KnownILCompilerPack Update
    covers the Native-AOT toolchain packs
    (runtime.<rid>.Microsoft.DotNet.ILCompiler +
    Microsoft.NETCore.App.Runtime.NativeAOT.<rid>) that no runtime-pack lever reaches.

    SCOPE (deliberately NOT pinned): KnownAppHostPack + KnownCrossgen2Pack stay at
    the SDK band default (net10 = 10.0.8). The apphost launcher stub and the
    crossgen2 R2R tool are not managed CoreCLR and are not in the #1815 CVE
    cluster (all 9 advisories are managed-runtime), so they are out of scope here.

    WHY THIS FILE, AND WHY CONDITIONED
    Directory.Build.targets is imported AFTER each csproj body, so $(SelfContained)
    / $(PublishAot) are already set here — a Directory.Build.props import runs
    BEFORE the csproj body and would see them empty. The property condition scopes
    the pin to exactly the projects that bake a runtime pack:
      - src: Wep.Agent, Wep.Agent.Updater, Wep.Agent.SessionHelper, Wep.Shim,
             Wep.SandboxAgent, Wep.Detonation.Broker (all set SelfContained=true
             in-file; Wep.Agent, Wep.Agent.Updater and Wep.Shim publish
             PublishAot=true via the release command lines, but the SessionHelper,
             Updater and Shim copies inside the agent are JIT build output, #3380).
      - tools: the Wep.Tools.* verifiers / builders + ProxmoxResultExtractor
             (SelfContained=true in-file).
    It deliberately does NOT fire for the framework-dependent path:
    Wep.ControlPlane.{Web,Catalog} publish into the aspnet:10.0 Docker image with
    a self-contained:false switch (see src/Wep.ControlPlane.Web/Dockerfile) — the
    base image supplies the shared framework, so $(SelfContained) is false there and
    no floor is imposed on that build. Libraries and test projects are framework-
    dependent too and are likewise untouched, so this pin adds no
    "runtime >= 10.0.11 must be installed to run" requirement to local test runs.

    The Update targets share their Include identity across ALL of the SDK's
    KnownFrameworkReference bands (Microsoft.NETCore.App alone has 8 entries in
    BundledVersions.props, from net10.0 down through netcoreapp3.0), so each Update
    touches all bands — inert, since no project in this repo targets < net10, so
    only the net10.0 band is ever resolved and the other bands' edits never apply.
    (A metadata-batched Condition like '%(TargetFramework)'=='net10.0' is NOT legal
    in a static ItemGroup — MSB4191 — and a Target-time edit is not reliably visible
    to restore, which is where the runtime-pack PackageDownload set is computed.)

    !! STALENESS — READ BEFORE TRUSTING THESE NUMBERS !!
    $(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion) is an MSBuild value, NOT a NuGet
    PackageReference. Dependabot does NOT bump MSBuild properties, and the only
    thing that catches a stale value is the floor assertion below (which fails the
    build if the RESOLVED runtime pack is under the floor). BUMP THIS ONE PROPERTY
    EACH .NET PATCH TUESDAY to the newest patched 10.0.x runtime — the Updates and
    the assertion all read it, so it is the single source of truth. See #1815 for
    the full mechanism and the B+C alternative (SDK-band floor + build guard) if
    SDK-driven auto-patching is ever preferred over a hand-bumped pin.

    #4916: .github/workflows/dotnet-runtime-bump.yml now opens the bump PR daily.
    It reads Microsoft's 10.0 release metadata every day, so an out-of-band
    release is caught as well as patch Tuesday's, and once every pack this pin
    moves is on nuget.org and the sdk and aspnet images carry the new runtime,
    its GitHub App opens one PR that moves this property, the platform image's
    ARG WEP_MIN_RUNTIME and both base digests together. Until the App is set up
    it opens an issue with those edits instead. The hand bump above stays the
    fallback whenever no PR arrives: ci/dotnet/Set-DotnetRuntimeBump.ps1 makes
    the same four edits.
  -->

  <!-- #1815: single source of truth for BOTH the pin (Updates below) and the floor
       (assertion below). Bump this every .NET patch Tuesday; since #4916
       dotnet-runtime-bump.yml opens that PR daily (see STALENESS above). -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion>10.0.14</WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- #1815: pin the self-contained runtime packs to the floor. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(SelfContained)' == 'true' or '$(PublishAot)' == 'true'">
    <KnownFrameworkReference Update="Microsoft.NETCore.App"
                             LatestRuntimeFrameworkVersion="$(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion)"
                             DefaultRuntimeFrameworkVersion="$(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion)" />
    <KnownFrameworkReference Update="Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App"
                             LatestRuntimeFrameworkVersion="$(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion)"
                             DefaultRuntimeFrameworkVersion="$(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion)" />
    <KnownFrameworkReference Update="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App"
                             LatestRuntimeFrameworkVersion="$(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion)"
                             DefaultRuntimeFrameworkVersion="$(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- #1815: pin the Native-AOT toolchain (ILCompiler pack + the
       Microsoft.NETCore.App.Runtime.NativeAOT.<rid> runtime pack that shares its
       version) to the same floor. Only Wep.Agent + the agent-family exes reach
       this (PublishAot on the release command line). -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(PublishAot)' == 'true'">
    <KnownILCompilerPack Update="Microsoft.DotNet.ILCompiler"
                         ILCompilerPackVersion="$(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    #1815 floor assertion: PROVE the pin took effect. The Updates above silently
    no-op if a future SDK renames the KnownFrameworkReference item (the resolved
    runtime pack then reverts to the band default, e.g. 10.0.8 — below the floor),
    and a hand-bumped floor could drift ahead of a stale pin. Without a check the
    vulnerable runtime returns while LOOKING fixed. This target reads the CONCRETE
    resolved Microsoft.NETCore.App runtime-pack version (RuntimePackVersion on the
    ResolvedFrameworkReference item, populated by ResolveFrameworkReferences) and
    fails the build if it is below the floor. Gated identically to the pin, so it
    never fires for framework-dependent builds; and it is silent on the happy path
    (resolved == floor). Version-aware compare — string compare is wrong
    ('10.0.9' > '10.0.11' lexically).
  -->
  <!--
    Scope (fix for the platform-v0.147.0 release failure, 2026-08-27): the
    SelfContained / PublishAot switches arrive as GLOBAL properties on the release
    command line (`dotnet publish … /p:SelfContained=true /p:PublishAot=…`) and
    MSBuild forwards global properties to every ProjectReference, so the pin's
    condition is also true inside the agent's referenced LIBRARIES (e.g.
    Wep.AgentCollector.Contract). A library never resolves a runtime pack — it
    ships no runtime — so its ResolvedFrameworkReference carries no
    RuntimePackVersion and the "could not read" branch below fired, killing the
    first release after #1815 (run 33113540358). The floor is a property of the
    project that PRODUCES the self-contained runtime, so the assertion runs only
    for Exe/WinExe projects; libraries still receive the KnownFrameworkReference
    pin above (harmless) but are not asked to prove a runtime they do not ship.
    build-and-test never publishes self-contained, which is why CI could not see
    this — reproduce locally with
    `dotnet build src/Wep.Agent/Wep.Agent.csproj -c Release -r win-x64 /p:SelfContained=true /p:PublishAot=true`.
  -->
  <Target Name="_WepAssertRuntimePackMeetsFloor"
          AfterTargets="ResolveFrameworkReferences"
          Condition="('$(SelfContained)' == 'true' or '$(PublishAot)' == 'true') and ('$(OutputType)' == 'Exe' or '$(OutputType)' == 'WinExe')">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_WepResolvedNetCoreRuntimePackVersion>@(ResolvedFrameworkReference->WithMetadataValue('Identity','Microsoft.NETCore.App')->'%(RuntimePackVersion)')</_WepResolvedNetCoreRuntimePackVersion>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- Happy-path evidence line: lets a release log prove the floor was checked
         for each shipped exe (and which project it was checked on). -->
    <Message Importance="high"
             Condition="'$(_WepResolvedNetCoreRuntimePackVersion)' != ''"
             Text="#1815 runtime-pack floor: $(MSBuildProjectName) resolved Microsoft.NETCore.App runtime pack $(_WepResolvedNetCoreRuntimePackVersion) (floor $(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion))" />

    <!-- Empty = the SDK item/metadata contract this assertion reads has changed,
         so the pin can no longer be verified. Fail loud rather than ship blind. -->
    <Error Condition="'$(_WepResolvedNetCoreRuntimePackVersion)' == ''"
           Text="#1815 floor assertion could not read the resolved Microsoft.NETCore.App runtime-pack version (@(ResolvedFrameworkReference)-&gt;RuntimePackVersion) for the self-contained/AOT project $(MSBuildProjectFile). The SDK item/metadata contract likely changed; the KnownFrameworkReference pin in Directory.Build.targets can no longer be verified. Re-verify the pin mechanism before shipping." />

    <Error Condition="'$(_WepResolvedNetCoreRuntimePackVersion)' != '' and $([System.Version]::Parse('$(_WepResolvedNetCoreRuntimePackVersion)').CompareTo($([System.Version]::Parse('$(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion)')))) &lt; 0"
           Text="#1815 VULNERABLE RUNTIME: the resolved Microsoft.NETCore.App runtime pack is $(_WepResolvedNetCoreRuntimePackVersion), below the required floor $(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion), for the self-contained/AOT project $(MSBuildProjectFile). Either the KnownFrameworkReference pin in Directory.Build.targets did not take effect (SDK item renamed?) or $(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion) was bumped without a matching runtime pack being available. Repair the pin so the resolved runtime is at least $(WepMinimumRuntimeFrameworkVersion)." />
  </Target>

</Project>
